From 8b7e71b34f602385a68358dae3ee29e6be96ba91 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Lumnus <lumnus.org@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 02:12:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/8] B70-Q27-0003: [B70][MoE] Asynchronous hot/cold expert
 swap (B70_MOE_HOTCOLD_ASYNC_SWAP=1)

The swapper exchanged a hot slot and a cold row in place on the forward
stream and then synchronized the host, so each tick stalled decode for
the copies plus the host round trip: 160-940 ms per 5 s tick after a
domain shift on the 2-card Flash-Next (150-200 experts per tick).

With B70_MOE_HOTCOLD_ASYNC_SWAP=1 a swap of hot a (slot i) and cold b
(row j) becomes a cycle of phases, at most one per runner step, none of
which waits on the host:
  snap   route counters -> pinned snapshot on the forward stream (no .cpu())
  copy1  side stream: a's bytes, slot i -> a spare cold row s
  flip1  forward stream, once copy1's event has completed (query()):
         maps a -> cold row s
  copy2  side stream, after flip1's event: b's bytes, row j -> slot i
  flip2  forward stream, once copy2 has completed: maps b -> slot i;
         row j becomes the spare after flip2's event
A row is written only while no map references it and after every
forward that referenced it, and the maps flip only after the bytes are
complete, so every expert stays routable with whole bytes at every step.
Weights and maps are written in place (maps from pinned staging), so the
captured graphs read them unchanged; nothing is recaptured. The cold
prefetcher's staged layers are invalidated at every flip.

The cold tensors carry B70_MOE_HOTCOLD_ASYNC_SPARE free rows per layer
(default the swap budget; zeroed, unreferenced; ~1 GiB pinned per rank
at 8 x 48 layers on Flash-Next TP2), which also cap the swaps per layer
and cycle. The cold XpuFusedMoe counts them as experts with no rows.
The in-place exchange's VRAM temporaries are not allocated in this mode.
One log line at setup; per cycle 'B70 hot/cold swap rank R: N experts
in L layers async: wall over S steps, host ms'.

Unset (default), the synchronous swap is unchanged: the scoring moved
into _moves() and the spare rows are 0. The CPU-only test
(tests/model_executor/test_b70_hotcold_async_swap.py, torch.xpu
stubbed) shows it step for step identical to the synchronous swap on the same
traffic (maps and bytes, 160 ticks, 252 swaps), and with the switch on
that every expert is routed exactly once to its own bytes after each of
508 runner steps, with no host synchronize and the same convergence.
